---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const eqCfg = {
  curves: [
    { expr: '0.4*x/(1 + x)', label: 'products forming', color: '#d9480f', width: 2.2 },
    { expr: '0.4 - 0.4*x/(1 + x)', label: 'reactants remaining', color: '#475569' },
  ],
  x: { min: 0, max: 8, label: 'time →' },
  y: { min: 0, max: 0.45, label: 'amount' },
  crossings: true,
  height: 340,
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/chemistry/intermediate/chemical-equilibrium">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Most reactions run both ways, and they <strong>settle into a truce, not a finish line</strong>.
    Forward reaction makes products; the reverse reaction consumes them; when the two rates equal,
    concentrations freeze — <em>dynamic equilibrium</em>. Frozen does not mean equal: the balance
    point depends on the reaction's own constants and on whatever you do to the system next.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="reactants and products converging — where the curves cross is the equilibrium mixture" widget="plotter" config={eqCfg} />

  <p>
    The curves swap slopes and settle at fixed levels: reaction still roaring in both directions,
    but with no net change. That's why Haber-process ammonia factories can't push reactions to
    completion — and why they instead recycle unreacted gas endlessly.
  </p>

  <h2>Le Chatelier's principle: poke the balance</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Add a reagent → the system consumes it.</strong> Breathing removes CO₂ from blood, pulling the carbonic-acid equilibrium and shifting blood chemistry — hyperventilate and you feel dizzy for exactly this reason.</li>
    <li><strong>Squeeze (raise pressure) → the side with fewer gas molecules wins.</strong> N₂ + 3H₂ ⇌ 2NH₃ drops 4 molecules to 2 — high pressure favours ammonia, the whole basis of fertiliser production (Haber, 1913).</li>
    <li><strong>Heat helps the endothermic direction.</strong> If forward reaction absorbs heat, heating pushes products up. Ammonia synthesis releases heat, so it runs better cold — but cold is slow: industry lives on the compromise.</li>
    <li><strong>Catalysts change speed, never position.</strong> They reach equilibrium faster without moving it — the most-misquoted fact in the subject.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Le Chatelier (1884); Haber–Bosch process (1913); OpenStax, Chemistry 2e (Fundamental Equilibrium Concepts).</p>
</TopicPage>
